  [1mB. [38;5;153msession.js[39m + [38;5;153mauth.js[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes pure DOM wiring[0m — same [38;5;153msession.js[39m, plus [38;5;153mlogin()[39m and [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m move into
  [38;5;153mauth.js[39m. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m shrinks to reading inputs and calling imported functions.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m both real units become importable and testable without a DOM, and when the stub becomes a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m the
  change is confined to [38;5;153mauth.js[39m. Costs one more file and a larger diff than the request literally implies.
  [3mWins when:[0m you want tests, or you expect the backend to land soon. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. A generic [38;5;153mstore.js[39m with [38;5;153muserId[39m as its first key[0m — a general key/value app-state wrapper over localStorage; session
  identity is one entry among future ones.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m flexible for state you haven't specified yet, but it's speculative generality for a single field, and a
  generic [38;5;153mget('userId')[39m loses the typed, named interface that makes [38;5;153msession.js[39m self-documenting.
  [3mWins when:[0m you already know several unrelated pieces of state are coming. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m You scoped this to store-and-read, and A delivers exactly that with one new module and a clean
  interface for the later forms. I'd discard C outright — it's YAGNI, building a generic container for one known value.
  B is the right call [3monly if[0m you want [38;5;153mlogin()[39m under test; that's genuinely tied to the tooling question below, so I'll
  ask them together.

  To be explicit about the security boundary in all three: what goes into localStorage is the opaque [38;5;153muserId[39m and nothing
  else. No password, no token. localStorage is readable by any script on the origin, so this is a deliberate limit, not
  an oversight.
